<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Nassau-Siegen</h1><p class="st-meta">1606 CE – 1743 CE · capital Siegen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iron-mining Nassau principality in the Siegerland, divided for a century between Catholic and Protestant branches of the house.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-count-of-nassau-dillenburg">John VI</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau-dillenburg">Nassau-Dillenburg</a> in 1606, his lands were divided and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vii-count-of-nassau-siegen">John VII</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>. Siegen was the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-paul-rubens">Peter Paul Rubens</a> in 1577, when his parents lived there in exile.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegerland">Siegerland's</a> iron ore and forges made it relatively prosperous. The house split confessionally: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-viii-count-of-nassau-siegen">John VIII</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and his line held part of the land while the Reformed line held the rest, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a> had two castles, the Upper and Lower. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-maurits-of-nassau-siegen">Johan Maurits of Nassau-Siegen</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-brazil">Dutch Brazil</a>, belonged to the Reformed branch. Counts became princes in 1652 and 1664.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Reformed line died out in 1734 and the Catholic line in 1743, and the territory passed to Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-prince-of-orange">William IV of Orange-Nassau</a> as part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-nassau-diez">Nassau-Diez</a>. It later went to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-berg">Grand Duchy of Berg</a> and in 1815 to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side">